WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.920
Manual and degraded operation can preserve a critical service when automation, communications,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.929
or operator visibility is unavailable.

00:00:06.929 --> 00:00:10.946
The fallback may involve local control, reduced throughput, alternate equipment,

00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:14.561
physical rounds, temporary communications, or a controlled process shutdown.

00:00:14.561 --> 00:00:19.382
Manual operation is realistic only when the process was designed for it

00:00:19.382 --> 00:00:21.792
and people can perform it safely.

00:00:21.792 --> 00:00:25.407
Some modern systems lack local controls, accessible measurements, staffing,

00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:27.817
or response time for manual control.

00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:33.441
A plan should never assume that operators can replace fast automated logic by improvisation.

00:00:33.441 --> 00:00:37.458
Define entry conditions, authority, minimum staffing, equipment limits, safety checks,

00:00:37.458 --> 00:00:39.467
communications, recordkeeping, and exit criteria.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:43.082
Degraded mode may change alarm coverage, interlocks, quality assurance,

00:00:43.082 --> 00:00:47.501
environmental monitoring, or emergency response, so accepted limits must be explicit.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:52.723
Provide paper procedures and offline documentation for situations when normal systems are unavailable.

00:00:52.723 --> 00:00:56.338
Include current diagrams, operating ranges, contact lists, check sheets,

00:00:56.338 --> 00:01:00.355
startup and shutdown sequences, and locations of tools and spares.

00:01:00.355 --> 00:01:04.372
Protect sensitive copies while keeping them accessible during an outage.

00:01:04.372 --> 00:01:07.586
Train operators and maintenance personnel through safe exercises.

00:01:07.586 --> 00:01:10.800
Consider shift coverage, fatigue, travel restrictions, vendor availability,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.415
and how long a manual mode can be sustained.

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:19.637
A fallback that works for thirty minutes may not work for two days.

00:01:19.637 --> 00:01:21.646
Test assumptions and capture lessons.

00:01:21.646 --> 00:01:26.466
If manual operation is unsafe or unrealistic, continuity planning should emphasize redundancy,

00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:29.278
rapid replacement, alternate service, or safe shutdown.

00:01:29.278 --> 00:01:34.500
Honest limits are more valuable than a reassuring procedure that cannot be executed.
